    "audioUrl": "NCE/NCE2/96.The Dead Return.mp3",
    "lrcUrl": "NCE/NCE2/96.The Dead Return.lrc",
    "lrcText": "[al:新概念英语第二册]\n[ar:山海作品推荐]\n[ti:The Dead Return]\n[by:山海作品推荐]\n[00:11.01]What happens to the lanterns at the end of the festival? | 节日结束后，那些灯笼会怎么样？\n[00:17.38]A Festival for the Dead is held once a year in Japan. | 日本每年举行一次亡灵节。\n[00:22.72]This festival is a cheerful occasion, | 这个节日是个欢乐的时刻，\n[00:26.05]for on this day, the dead are said to return to their homes and they are welcomed by the living. | 因为据说在这一天，逝者会回到他们的家园，受到生者的欢迎。\n[00:34.36]As they are expected to be hungry after their long journey, food is laid out for them. | 由于他们长途跋涉后会感到饥饿，人们会为他们摆放食物。\n[00:41.81]Specially-made lanterns are hung outside each house to help the dead to find their way. | 每家每户外面都挂着特制的灯笼，帮助逝者找到回家的路。\n[00:49.85]All night long, people dance and sing. | 人们通宵达旦地载歌载舞。\n[00:54.07]In the early morning the food that had been laid out for the dead | 清晨，为逝者摆放的食物\n[00:59.49]is thrown into a river or into the sea as it is considered unlucky for anyone living to eat it. | 被扔进河里或海里，因为活人吃了被认为是不吉利的。\n[01:09.25]In towns that are near the sea, the tiny lanterns which had been hung in the streets the night before, | 在沿海城镇，头天晚上挂在街上的小灯笼，\n[01:17.84]are placed into the water when the festival is over. | 节日结束后会被放入水中。\n[01:22.15]Thousands of lanterns slowly drift out to sea guiding the dead on their return journey to the other world. | 数千盏灯笼缓缓地漂向大海，指引逝者返回另一个世界。\n[01:31.58]This is a moving spectacle, for crowds of people | 这是一幅感人的景象，因为成群的人们\n[01:36.07]stand on the shore watching the lanterns drifting away until they can be seen no more. | 站在岸边，看着灯笼渐渐远去，直到它们完全消失不见。",
    "hasAudio": true,
    "hasLrc": true,
    "source": "default"
