    "lrcText": "[al:新概念英语第三册]\n[ar:山海作品推荐]\n[ti:Justice was Done]\n[by:山海作品推荐]\n[00:00.43]Lesson 35 | 第35课\n[00:02.64]Justice was done | 正义得以伸张\n[00:10.56]The word 'justice' is given two different meanings in the text. | 在课文中，“正义”一词被赋予了两种不同的含义。\n[00:15.28]What is the distinction between them? | 它们之间有什么区别呢？\n[00:19.85]The word justice is usually associated with courts of law. | “正义”一词通常与法庭联系在一起。\n[00:25.49]We might say that justice has been done when a man's innocence or guilt has been proved beyond doubt. | 当一个人的清白或罪行被无可置疑地证明时，我们可能会说正义得到了伸张。\n[00:33.15]Justice is part of the complex machinery of the law. | 正义是法律复杂机制的一部分。\n[00:37.75]Those who seek it, undertake an arduous journey and can never be sure that they will find it. | 追求正义的人会踏上一段艰难的旅程，而且永远无法确定他们是否会找到它。\n[00:44.88]Judges, however wise or eminent, are human and can make mistakes. | 法官，无论多么明智或杰出，都是人，也可能犯错误。\n[00:51.18]There are rare instances when justice almost ceases to be an abstract concept. | 在极少数情况下，正义几乎不再是一个抽象的概念。\n[00:58.16]Reward or punishment are meted out quite independent of human interference. | 奖励或惩罚在完全没有人类干预的情况下被施加。\n[01:04.30]At such times, justice acts like a living force. | 在这种时候，正义就像一股活生生的力量。\n[01:09.44]When we use a phrase like 'it serves him right', | 当我们使用“活该”这样的短语时，\n[01:13.51]we are, in part, admitting that a certain set of circumstances has enabled justice to act of its own accord. | 我们部分承认了某种情况使正义得以自然而然地发挥作用。\n[01:23.84]When a thief was caught on the premises of a large jewellery store one morning, | 一天早上，当一个小偷在一家大型珠宝店的院子里被抓到时，\n[01:29.48]the shop assistants must have found it impossible to resist the temptation to say 'it serves him right'. | 店员们一定觉得很难抵制住说“活该”的冲动。\n[01:37.67]The shop was an old converted house with many large, disused fireplaces and tall, narrow chimneys. | 这家店是一座由老房子改建而成的，有很多大的废弃壁炉和又高又窄的烟囱。\n[01:46.07]Towards midday, a girl heard a muffled cry coming from behind one of the walls. | 快到中午时，一个女孩听到墙后传来一阵低沉的哭声。\n[01:52.86]As the cry was repeated several times, | 哭声重复了几次之后，\n[01:55.79]she ran to tell the manager who promptly rang up the fire brigade. | 她跑去告诉经理，经理立刻给消防队打了电话。\n[02:01.37]The cry had certainly come from one of the chimneys, | 哭声确实是从一个烟囱里传出来的，\n[02:04.65]but as there were so many of them, the firemen could not be certain which one it was. | 但由于烟囱太多，消防员无法确定是哪一个。\n[02:12.25]They located the right chimney by tapping at the walls and listening for the man's cries. | 他们通过敲击墙壁并聆听那个人的哭声来确定正确的烟囱。\n[02:19.67]After chipping through a wall which was eighteen inches thick, | 在凿穿一堵18英寸厚的墙后，\n[02:24.23]they found that a man had been trapped in the chimney. | 他们发现一个人被困在烟囱里了。\n[02:28.40]As it was extremely narrow, the man was unable to move, | 由于烟囱非常狭窄，那个人无法动弹，\n[02:33.21]but the fire fighters were eventually able to free him by cutting a huge hole in the wall. | 但消防员最终通过在墙上凿开一个大洞，成功地解救了他。\n[02:40.91]The sorry-looking, blackened figure that emerged, | 那个狼狈不堪、浑身漆黑的人影出现后，\n[02:44.98]admitted at once that he had tried to break into the shop during the night but had got stuck in the chimney. | 立刻承认他曾试图在夜间闯入商店，但被卡在了烟囱里。\n[02:53.22]He had been there for nearly ten hours. | 他已经在里面待了将近十个小时。\n[02:57.25]Justice had been done even before the man was handed over to the police. | 甚至在将那个人移交给警方之前，正义就已经得到了伸张。",
    "hasAudio": true,
    "hasLrc": true,
    "source": "default"
