    "lrcText": "[al:新概念英语第三册]\n[ar:山海作品推荐]\n[ti:The Power of the Press]\n[by:山海作品推荐]\n[00:00.17]Lesson 45 | 第45课\n[00:02.26]The power of the press | 新闻界的力量\n[00:09.99]Does the writer think the parents were lucky or unlucky to gain prosperity in this way? Why? | 作者认为这对父母以这种方式获得财富是幸运还是不幸？为什么？\n[00:20.22]In democratic countries any efforts to restrict the freedom of the press are rightly condemned. | 在民主国家，任何限制新闻自由的努力都理应受到谴责。\n[00:28.59]However, this freedom can easily be abused. | 然而，这种自由很容易被滥用。\n[00:33.74]Stories about people often attract far more public attention than political events. | 关于人物的故事常常比政治事件更能吸引公众的注意力。\n[00:40.73]Though we may enjoy reading about the lives of others, | 尽管我们可能喜欢阅读别人的生活故事，\n[00:44.94]it is extremely doubtful whether we would equally enjoy reading about ourselves. | 但我们是否同样喜欢阅读关于我们自己的故事，这一点是极度怀疑的。\n[00:51.76]Acting on the contention that facts are sacred, | 记者们秉持着“事实神圣不可侵犯”的论点，\n[00:56.16]reporters can cause untold suffering to individuals by publishing details about their private lives. | 通过刊登个人私生活的细节，给他们造成了无法言喻的痛苦。\n[01:05.83]Newspapers exert such tremendous influence that they can not only bring about major changes to the lives of ordinary people but can even overthrow a government. | 报纸施加着巨大的影响力，它们不仅能给普通人的生活带来巨大变化，甚至可以推翻一个政府。\n[01:19.22]The story of a poor family that acquired fame and fortune overnight, dramatically illustrates the power of the press. | 一个贫困家庭一夜之间获得名利的故事，戏剧性地说明了新闻界的力量。\n[01:28.30]The family lived in Aberdeen, a small town of 23, 000 inhabitants in South Dakota. | 这个家庭住在南达科他州一个拥有23,000居民的小镇阿伯丁。\n[01:36.02]As the parents had five children, life was a perpetual struggle against poverty. | 由于这对父母有五个孩子，生活一直是与贫困的无休止的斗争。\n[01:42.12]They were expecting their sixth child and were faced with even more pressing economic problems. | 他们正期待着第六个孩子，面临着更加紧迫的经济问题。\n[01:49.51]If they had only had one more child, the fact would have passed unnoticed. | 如果他们只多生一个孩子，这件事就会不为人知。\n[01:55.53]They would have continued to struggle against economic odds and would have lived in obscurity. | 他们会继续在经济困境中挣扎，并默默无闻地生活。\n[02:02.51]But they suddenly became the parents of quintuplets, four girls and a boy, an event which radically changed their lives. | 但他们突然成了五胞胎的父母，四个女孩和一个男孩，这一事件彻底改变了他们的生活。\n[02:12.99]The day after the birth of the five children, an aeroplane arrived in Aberdeen bringing sixty reporters and photographers. | 孩子们出生后的第二天，一架飞机抵达阿伯丁，带来了六十名记者和摄影师。\n[02:22.41]The rise to fame was swift. | 成名之路迅速。\n[02:24.75]Television cameras and newspapers carried the news to everyone in the country. | 电视摄像机和报纸将这一消息传遍了全国各地。\n[02:30.03]Newspapers and magazines offered the family huge sums for the exclusive rights to publish stories and photographs. | 报纸和杂志为独家刊登这些故事和照片的权利，向这个家庭提供了巨额报酬。\n[02:38.52]Gifts poured in not only from unknown people, | 礼物源源不断地涌来，不仅来自陌生人，\n[02:42.22]but from baby food and soap manufacturers who wished to advertise their products. | 也来自希望宣传产品的婴儿食品和肥皂制造商。\n[02:48.83]The old farmhouse the family lived in was to be replaced by a new $500, 000 home. | 这家人住的老农舍将被一座价值50万美元的新房子取代。\n[02:56.83]Reporters kept pressing for interviews so lawyers had to be employed to act as spokesmen for the family at press conferences. | 记者们不停地要求采访，所以不得不雇佣律师在新闻发布会上担任这个家庭的发言人。\n[03:06.84]While the five babies were still quietly sleeping in oxygen tents in a hospital nursery, | 当五个婴儿还在医院育婴室的氧气帐篷里安静地睡觉时，\n[03:13.58]their parents were paying the price for fame. | 他们的父母却在为名声付出代价。\n[03:17.27]It would never again be possible for them to lead normal lives. | 他们再也无法过上正常的生活了。\n[03:22.20]They had become the victims of commercialization, for their names had acquired a market value. | 他们成了商业化的受害者，因为他们的名字已经有了市场价值。\n[03:30.25]Instead of being five new family members, these children had immediately become a commodity. | 这些孩子不再是五个新的家庭成员，而是立刻变成了商品。",
    "hasAudio": true,
    "hasLrc": true,
    "source": "default"
